# The SanusOS rice

Everything here is installed to `/usr/share/sanus/rice/` and applied to a home
directory by `sanus-rice`. The package that owns this directory is
`sanus-theme/sanus-theme`.

## The look

One idea, applied everywhere: **black, translucent, blurred, with white
highlights over a single ice-blue glow.** Nothing is grey "for contrast";
surfaces are black at ~55% opacity and let the compositor blur the wallpaper
through them.

| Token | Value | Used by |
|---|---|---|
| base | `#050508` | terminals, wallpaper |
| surface | `rgba(10,10,12,0.55)` | waybar, wofi, mako |
| text | `#f0f0f5` | everywhere |
| text-dim | `#9aa3ad` | secondary labels |
| accent | `#f0f0f5` | focus, active workspace, icons, cursor |
| accent-glow | `#7dd3fc` | selection rings, shadows, wallpaper |
| urgent | `#f87171` | errors, critical battery |

The palette is defined once in `hypr/sanus-colors.conf`, once as CSS variables
at the top of `waybar/style.css`, and once inline in `wofi/style.css`. If you
change it, change all three — they are deliberately not generated from a
template, because a template that fails silently is worse than three files.

## Where transparency comes from

Hyprland does the blur, not the individual apps:

```
decoration { blur { enabled = true; size = 7; passes = 3 } }
layerrule = blur, waybar
layerrule = blur, wofi
```

Applications only need to *be* translucent — `background_opacity 0.85` in
kitty, `alpha=0.86` in foot, an alpha channel in the waybar CSS. If a window is
opaque, blur does nothing to it, which is exactly what we want for Steam and
games.

Games are explicitly excluded from every effect:

```
windowrulev2 = opacity 1.0 override 1.0, class:^(steam)$
windowrulev2 = noblur,   class:^(steam|gamescope|wine)$
windowrulev2 = norounding, class:^(gamescope)$
```

## Applying it

```bash
sanus-rice --user     # apply the rice to $HOME
sanus-rice --system   # apply to /etc/skel and every user in /home (root)
sanus-rice --check    # show what would change, change nothing
sanus-rice --force    # overwrite files you have modified
emerge --config sanus-theme/sanus-theme   # same as --system --force
```

`sanus-rice` never overwrites a file it did not write. Every file it manages
carries a marker line; if the marker is missing it assumes you took the file
over and leaves it alone (unless `--force`).

## Adding a machine-local tweak

Do not edit `/usr/share/sanus/rice` — it is replaced on upgrade. Instead:

* Hyprland: add `~/.config/hypr/local.conf` and add
  `source = ~/.config/hypr/local.conf` at the bottom of your `hyprland.conf`.
* Waybar: put a second bar in `~/.config/waybar/config.jsonc` under a different
  `name`, or override a module in `~/.config/waybar/style.css`.
* Everything else: the file is yours once you edit it.

## Wallpaper

`sanus-wallpaper` renders `wallpapers/sanus-wallpaper.svg` to PNG at install
time (it needs `rsvg-convert` or ImageMagick), writes it to
`/usr/share/sanus/wallpaper/`, and tells hyprpaper to reload. It also writes a
darker variant used by the lock screen.

```bash
sanus-wallpaper              # regenerate from the shipped SVG
sanus-wallpaper --dark       # regenerate the dark variant only
sanus-wallpaper --next       # cycle a random accent hue (regenerates SVG)
```
